\end{center}
The owner is a record identifier. It is not interpreted as a word in a
stabilizer.

Write the corresponding involutions as $a,b,c$ and put
\[
 r=ab,\qquad s=bc,\qquad K=\gen{r,s},\qquad J=\gen{a,b,c}.
\]
The identification of this action with the named simple group, and the
membership $a,b,c\in G$, are imported from the accepted original model.
The middle involution is the product of the three commuting involutions
specified by the exact witness triangle. Each of those involutions is
defined by the accepted equivariant point bridge. Thus the middle is
reconstructed in the same action as the two endpoints; an independently
chosen permutation model is not used.

The other imported premise is the complete maximal subgroup theorem of
Kleidman and Wilson~\cite{KW,KWc}. In its list of fourteen conjugacy
classes, no maximal subgroup has order divisible by both $11$ and $13$.
The earlier common argument, \emph{Generation in Fi22 from two supplied
prime cycles}, records the full list, the orders and the correction.
The groups whose orders are divisible by $11$ have types
$2.\mathrm{U}_6(2)$, $2^{10}{:}\mathrm{M}_{22}$ and $\mathrm{M}_{12}$;
those divisible by $13$ have types $\mathrm{O}_7(3)$ and
${}^2\mathrm{F}_4(2)'$. These sets of types are disjoint.

