\begin{lemma}
If an element $w$ of a finite group acting on a set has a cycle of prime
length $p$, then $p$ divides the order of $w$ and hence the order of every
subgroup containing $w$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $m$ be the order of $w$. The equality $w^m=1$ makes $w^m$ fix the
first point of the cycle. Its least positive return time under $w$ is
$p$, because all $p$ points are distinct and the last edge closes the
cycle. Therefore $p\mid m$. Lagrange's theorem gives the second
assertion. No assumption of faithfulness is needed for this implication.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}
Under the imported model, membership and maximal subgroup premises,
the marking in row $298$ satisfies
\[
 \gen{ab,bc}=G\qquad\hbox{and}\qquad\gen{a,b,c}=G.
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Both witness words lie in $K\leq G$. The two closed cycles and the lemma
show that $11\mid |K|$ and $13\mid |K|$. If $K<G$, finiteness places
$K$ in a maximal subgroup $M$ of $G$. Lagrange's theorem would then make
both primes divide $|M|$, contrary to the complete maximal subgroup
list. Hence $K=G$. Since $K\leq J\leq G$, we also have $J=G$.
\end{proof}

Only divisibility of the word orders is needed. The certificate does not
assert that the orders of the entire permutations are exactly $11$ and
$13$. Membership in the inner simple group is essential to the use of
the maximal subgroup theorem: the same argument with only membership
in $\operatorname{Aut}(G)$ would not suffice.

\section{Transfer and scope}

For the fixed ordered endpoints let
$H=C_{\operatorname{Aut}(G)}(a,c)$. If $h\in H$, then
\[
 \gen{a,b^h,c}=\gen{a,b,c}^{\,h}=G,
\]
because $G$ is normal in its automorphism group. Generation is therefore
constant on the corresponding $H$ orbit of middles. The accepted
equivariant product map identifies the two witness triangles in each
fibre with the same middle involution. Choosing the partner triangle
consequently leaves the subgroup unchanged. Applying these two facts
uses the already accepted full $H$, orbit and two-element fibre
identifications for this witness bank; this calculation supplies no new
orbit partition or fibre count.

The other six rows in the numerical batch have no accepted generation
or nongeneration conclusion. This proposition supplies generation for
row $298$ and its previously identified transfer scope. A separate
intersection argument is required before granting intersection-property
credit. Full automorphism and reversal counts, other selectors and the
complete manuscript classification remain separate obligations.

